---
import config from '../config.json';
import { content, threads, value } from '../lib/content.ts';
import { PLATFORMS } from '../lib/rsvp.mjs';
import { newTabQualifier } from '../lib/links.mjs';

/*
 * WHAT THIS SECTION REPLACED (2026-11-03). It used to be six cards with checkmarks — "Expert Guidance",
 * "Collaborative Learning", "Regular Meetups", "Study Resources", "Diverse Community", "Growth Mindset" —
 * under "Why Join Our Math Book Club? / Discover the benefits of collaborative learning and mathematical
 * exploration". Not one of those sentences came from `src/content.json`, from a source, or from the club:
 * "Learn from experienced mathematicians and educators", "Access to curated learning materials, problem
 * sets, and recommended readings" and "Monthly in-person study sessions" describe services this club does
 * not offer. The operator on the same day: the page has "no humanity", it is "too professional", and on
 * whether the club has ever written anything — "Our club has not written anything." All of it is now on the
 * retired list in `scripts/check-content.mjs`, so pasting a template's benefit grid back in fails the build.
 *
 * WHAT IS HERE INSTEAD, AND WHY IT IS UNDATED (operator ruling, 2026-11-03): "I don't want the home page to
 * give anyone an idea that our groups interests are as narrow as what we have done historically, so I don't
 * want to name enough specific things to give enough information to allow a pattern to emerge." A reading
 * list is exactly such a pattern-maker: nine titles with dates on them let a stranger infer the shape of the
 * club's past and predict its future. So this is a set of open threads, not an archive — no dates, no
 * authors, no titles, no sequence, and no row of past topics. A thread that goes quiet gets edited out of
 * `src/content.json` rather than kept as a record. `threads._rule` in that file is the authority; this
 * comment is where the shape of it is visible to whoever edits the markup.
 *
 * The strings come from `src/content.json` (source: the operator's account of the club's Discord channel,
 * 2026-11-03), so a non-developer can add or drop a thread from a phone without touching markup. An item
 * with no `value` is a hole and renders nothing rather than a bracket (record 0010).
 *
 * The file name stays `WhyJoinUs.astro`: the section is still the answer to "why would I come", and the
 * design tooling (`design/gen-token-map.mjs`, `design/ratify-check.mjs`, `design/audit-references.mjs`)
 * names this path. Renaming it is a separate change with its own reference sweep.
 */
const items = threads();
/**
 * The cap on the home page (S042, second design read §5.4, 2026-11-14). Nine sentence-length bullets is
 * a wall; six in two columns reads as a table of contents. The remainder is not deleted — it is on the
 * page where the threads actually live, `/chat`, under `#threads` — because a "the rest of the list →"
 * link that lands on a page with no list is the same defect class as a button labelled "Details and
 * sign-up" that goes to Meetup (D273). The number is a presentation rule, not a content ruling: the
 * full set still lives in `src/content.json` and `threads()` still returns all of it.
 *
 * Two columns are already the shipped shape at `md` (768px) with `gap-x-8` (32px), which is DESIGN.md's
 * "32px between grid columns"; the read's "≥900px" is not adopted as a new breakpoint because Tailwind v4
 * would need `--breakpoint-900` and no gate here measures 900 — `check:render` and `check:space` measure
 * 1240, 640, 390 and 320, and a breakpoint nobody measures is a layout nobody has seen (D269's reasoning,
 * applied to a media query). The row rhythm the read asked for (24px row gap, 12px within a row) is
 * already the ruled one: DESIGN.md rules 8px row padding and 16px between rows (R6, D214), which is what
 * `py-2` on each `li` produces.
 */
// Round-8 review (2026-11-18), Home/Add "one sentence naming a human" and "one sentence of past".
// Both come from `src/content.json` and not from markup: they are claims about the club, and D415's
// rule is that a claim whose source is a grant ships with `asOf` and a `correction` path attached,
// which only a slot can do. The review's own copy line carries a literal `[host name]`; a bracket on
// the page is a hole in our knowledge and `check:content` fails a build that ships one, so the
// shipped sentence says "the host" (no roster in this tree names one) and the name comes back by
// editing the value. `pastLine` shipped EMPTY for the same reason the operator's 2026-11-03 ruling above
// gives: nothing here may sketch the club's past densely enough to let a pattern emerge, and no file
// in this tree names a first meeting - so the slot is the home for the operator's year, and today it
// paints nothing (the operator's ruling quoted two paragraphs above holds that emptiness, not a decision
// we minted here).
// 2026-11-19: it paints something now. The operator's vision letter (docs/the-authors-vision-2026-11-19.md
// §3) supplies one fact - "The first year was 2019" - and it lives in that slot, so this section prints it
// under `hostLine`. What the ruling above forbids is unchanged: one year, one sentence, no dense history a
// pattern could emerge from, and no derived age ("over seven years of mathematics") which would be a count
// with no source (D92). A bare year is not a calendar date to `check:dated-surfaces`, whose DATE_TEXT wants
// a month name or an ISO date, so this adds no date string and the ruled set of dated surfaces is untouched
// (D449, and the record's own fact lexicon gained a stated year the same day so the sentence is a fact and
// not worklist).
const hostLine = value(content.hostLine);
const pastLine = value(content.pastLine);
const VISIBLE = 6;
const shown = items.slice(0, VISIBLE);
const rest = items.length - shown.length;
---

{/* `py-8` (32px), not `py-12` (48px): the band air was ruled down on 2026-11-05 (D214, R6) and measured
    again on 2026-11-06, and round 18 ruled it down one step more on 2026-11-17 — the operator accepted the
    grill's row F (`no-new-art-less-air`), which prices 44.3k `mt-6` + 40.6k `mt-12` as 84.9k of section
    padding and asks whether the whole section can come off one step of the spacing scale. That row's evidence
    was the first composition row whose claim is LESS of the thing that made the emptiness rather than more
    colour, and it moved the fold 168px at all four widths; see DESIGN.md §Spacing and
    docs/reply-to-the-composition-review-round-18-2026-11-16.md. D214's one-step rule still holds for the
    hero and for `py-6 py-4` inside surfaces; it does not hold across bands any more, and this note says so
    rather than pretending the four bands still share one number.

    The section is a *named* landmark (D331, 2026-11-15): a `<section>` is only a region for assistive tech
    when it has an accessible name, and axe-core's `region` rule - moderate impact - called this band, the
    one top-level block on `/` that had no name, out-of-landmark content on both the built page at 390 and
    at 1440 @measured 2026-11-15 by `node design/axe-check.mjs`. The name is the section's own heading by
    reference, not a second copy of the words: `aria-labelledby` keeps one string in the page. `HorizonBand.astro`
    and `Schedule.astro` were already named this way; this is the last band brought up to the same rule. */}
<section class="bg-panel py-8" aria-labelledby="what-we-take-up">
  <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="max-w-measure">
      <h2 id="what-we-take-up" class="text-heading font-extrabold text-ink">
        What we take up
      </h2>
      {/* Round 17 (MS03), operator ruling 2026-11-09 (S017 B3): the syllabus reassurance is the hero's,
          so this opener keeps only the provenance the gate needs (D92) - where the list comes from - and
          stops restating it. The two sentences became one; nothing was invented.

          Round-4 brief §8 (2026-11-15, D344): the clause that used to close this line - "and none of them is a
          prerequisite for arriving" - is a reassurance, not a provenance, and it moves to /join's "Your first one".
          §8 forbade cutting it, so it travelled rather than evaporated. **Corrected 2026-11-19, because that
          sentence is no longer true of the build**: the copy that travelled to `/join` went on 2026-11-17
          (`723f7a4`, the D392 consolidation), and the copy that lived in `/chat`'s lede went on 2026-11-19 when the
          operator cleared the `check:copy-contract` hollow worklist by deletion - "(A) drop all four of those."
          (**S079**, **D453**). `grep -rlo "prerequisite for arriving" dist/client` prints nothing
          @measured 2026-11-19, so §8's "it travels rather than evaporates" is superseded for this clause by
          **D453**, and what survives of the reassurance is this line's own "Any of them can be dropped
          mid-sentence", which is `/`'s half and the one a reader meets first. What stays here is what the list's
          own source is, which is the half D92 needs. The protected room line at the bottom of this band is untouched. */}
      {/* Round-8 review (2026-11-18), the same Home/Revise row that asks for the lede split: this round prices
          the item as a number, so `/`'s longest sentence of its own prose must land under 20 words. This line
          opened at 23. The colon becomes a full stop; no word is added, moved or re-inflected, and the provenance
          D92 needs (where the list comes from) is still the sentence a reader meets first. */}
      <p class="mt-4 text-lede leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">
        Threads that are open in the club's chat. Any of them can be dropped mid-sentence, and the list changes
        when the chat changes.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* The fragment is not decoration: Astro's expression parser takes one expression per parenthesised
       block, so the list and the "rest of the list" link have to be one node (S042).
       Astro has no // in a template: a line starting with // inside a fragment is literal text and gets painted
       on the page - nine such lines reached the homepage in one build (D325). Every note in this file is a
       real comment, and `check:escapes` now fails when one is not. */}
    {items.length > 0 && (
      <>
      {/* No hairlines between the rows, on purpose, and there are now two reasons, only one of which is a
          measurement: the render gate fails a painted 1px line that is a border/outline instead of the
          ratified inset ring (inset-shadow-rule). The other reason used to be contrast — rule measured 2.88
          on this section's bg-panel ground (record 0018) — and D316 retired it: rule is 3.85 on panel
          @measured 2026-11-14 by node design/grill/palette-remap.mjs, so a hairline here would now pass. It
          stays absent because the label/gloss weight difference already does the grouping the rule was going
          to do. Rows sit on 8px padding with 8px above the list and 32px between the two columns (DESIGN.md,
          Spacing; the air above the list came off one more step on 2026-11-17 when row F shipped, S061). */}
      <ul class="mt-2 grid grid-cols-1 gap-x-8 md:grid-cols-2">
        {shown.map(({ label, gloss }) => (
          <li class="py-2 text-body leading-prose font-body">
            <span class="font-semibold text-ink">{label}</span>
            {gloss && <span class="text-ink-2"> — {gloss}</span>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      {rest > 0 && (
        // Six here, the rest on /chat (S042, read §5.4). The arrow is aria-hidden because the words
        // already say where the link goes. (A // in an expression is code; only a // inside a fragment is
        // painted - that is the D325 class check:escapes now catches.)
        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/chat#threads"
            class="text-label font-medium text-ink underline decoration-rule underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent"
          >
            the rest of the list <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      )}
      {/* Round-8 review, Home/Add: the two sentences the review asked for after the list, both from
          slots above. They sit after the pointer, not before it, so the list and its rest-link stay
          one node (S042) and what a reader meets last is a human, not another exit. */}
      {hostLine && (
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-measure text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{hostLine}</p>
      )}
      {pastLine && (
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-measure text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{pastLine}</p>
      )}
      </>
    )}

    {items.length === 0 && (
      // record 0010: a hole is a designed state, never a blank. If every thread slot is empty the section
      // says so and hands the reader the path where threads actually live.
      // The edge is the accent here, and that is DESIGN.md's caution grammar rather than a ground
      // measurement. This section paints bg-panel, and the measurement that used to justify an accent edge —
      // rule at 2.88 on a tint, under the 3.0 graphic bar, 2.74 on tint
      // (design/records/0018-a-hairline-on-a-tint-is-under-its-own-bar.md) — is gone: D316's rule reads 3.85
      // on panel and 3.10 on tint @measured 2026-11-14 by node design/grill/palette-remap.mjs. A rule ring
      // would now pass. What the box still cannot do is straddle two grounds (D149-3c) with no paper to move
      // it onto, because the whole section is a tint, so it keeps ONE ground on both sides: inset-shadow-accent
      // with an accent word, 6.07 on panel and 5.77 on paper, both over the 4.5 text bar. The state genuinely
      // is a caution: an empty list is a fact about this page's wiring, not about the club, and the sentence
      // says exactly that. check:render asserts every inset ring's pair, so if this branch ever renders it is
      // measured. mt-2, matching the list this notice stands in place of, so the two states of this one slot
      // do not sit on different grids. The notice's own px-4 py-3 stays: DESIGN.md puts notice padding at
      // 12/16, and R6 ruled about rows, not notices. Nothing in the pair's measurement covers this branch —
      // the shipped page always has threads — so it is a consistency edit, declared as one.
      <div class="mt-2 inset-shadow-accent rounded-control px-4 py-3 max-w-measure">
        <p class="text-body font-medium text-accent">Nothing is on the list right now.</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2">
          What the club is pulling on is decided in its chat, so an empty list here means the page has not
          been told yet, not that the club is quiet.
        </p>
      </div>
    )}

    {/* The closing line used to end "One evening is enough to tell whether this is your kind of room." Same
        sentence as the compact schedule band's lede, so it appeared twice on the landing page, and both copies
        promised the reader a verdict about their own taste on the club's behalf (operator, 2026-11-07: rewrite
        it). What the page can honestly say is what a meeting costs and what it does not require; what it cannot
        say is how the room will feel to you. That is said plainly instead of guessed at - and the sentence is
        now in one place (this band) rather than two, so the two surfaces cannot drift. D239. */}
    {/* D273: this sentence used to end "Meetup, where the sign-up is". That is a claim about one platform, and
        the schedule rows under it answer per date: a date can have a Meetup page, a guild scheduled event on
        Discord, or neither (src/lib/rsvp.mjs). The band's own link is still the group page - that is the path
        this band can carry - but the sentence no longer tells a visitor where to sign up, because the row that
        prints the date does. */}
    {/* Round-6 review (2026-11-16), Home/Remove (D389): "Whether the room is your kind of room is not something
        a page can tell you" leaves `/` entirely. The reviewer's own reading - "your best-written sentence, in
        your worst position" - is the peak-end rule: the last thing a deciding reader met was the site conceding
        what it cannot say. The sentence is not deleted; it moves to `/join`, the page a reader reaches after
        they have decided, where it sits beside the two things the club does rather than at the end of the page.
        If the MS32 photograph ever ships, the reviewer's own condition makes the sentence unnecessary, and the
        row in the reply doc records that trigger. */}
    {/* Round-6 review (2026-11-16), Home/Revise terminology (D386): "Four names for one page on one screen.
        Pick `dates` everywhere, matching the nav chip." The standing row used to call this page `the schedule`
        while the chip above it said `Dates`; the sentence now drops the name rather than coining a fifth one,
        because the thing it states is about the dates the page prints, not about the page's label. `check:naming`'s
        alias census over the built artifact prints 0 prose aliases for `/dates` after this (see the reply doc). */}
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-measure text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">
      Dates and places go up on{' '}
      <a
        class="underline hover:text-accent"
        href={config.URLs.meetupGroup}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >Meetup<span class="sr-only">{newTabQualifier('Meetup', PLATFORMS.meetup)}</span></a>, and every date we list links to the page the club posts its sign-up on.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
